{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfgaBnMQY9icfGu1G1xMZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I rewrote all three files for this repair attempt, and the candidate passes the self-check (`OK`). The self-check doesn't test novelty, so whether it clears the near-duplicate gate is still open.\n\n**Why it was rejected:** the first attempt let a person's tally-span sensitivity and switching weight be correlated. That only changes how people differ from each other. Averaged over participants, its predictions matched the incumbent `person_pattern_sensitivity_rule_built` to within an RMSE of 0.00125, under the 0.002 threshold.\n\n**New model:** `accumulated_switch_evidence_rule_built`, a refinement of the incumbent that changes how predictions depend on the stimulus. I took the correlation out. The one change is that a switching rate's distance from the ideal is now weighted by how many transitions the sequence has, counted against a six-flip sequence. So the same too-streaky or too-alternating rate counts about twice as much in an 8-flip sequence as in a 4-flip one. In the incumbent, switching counts the same at every length.\n\n**Novelty:** the weighting is fixed, not a fitted parameter, so the fit can't shrink it back to the incumbent. Predictions should therefore differ between short and long pairs on the 4–8-flip novelty pool. I estimate the difference at roughly 0.02–0.05 in `p_left`, well above the threshold, but I haven't measured it.\n\n**Self-check result:** it loads, has a finite log-probability, meets the data contract, and finished the short smoke fit with a finite ELPD-LOO of −3533.2. That fit flagged 10.7% of trials as unreliable for PSIS-LOO (Pareto k > 0.5). With 100 draws on one chain that may just be the short run, and convergence wasn't checked either. Admission's full fit will decide both.\n\n**Link to the critique:** it addresses the critique only indirectly. It predicts that agreement driven by switching grows with sequence length, which the incumbent can't produce. It does not directly model the extra spread between people on pairs where pattern structure can't act.\n\nI also checked whether to count plain palindromes (sequences that read the same backwards) as rule-built. I dropped the idea: in the data, people don't clearly avoid palindromes.\n\nFiles are in `/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run2/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop/iter_2/candidate_4_repair_1`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2637,"cache_read_input_tokens":95094,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2637,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6a704b9b-d7ea-4c5a-b004-c96040676111","uuid":"85b3ad0b-0d01-492f-8d29-fee52c9b3382","timestamp":"2026-10-04T04:33:56.674Z","request_id":"req_011CfgaBmHAhqppPbQaPr5yB"}
